   "subject": "Minimum number of items which can go together for calibration",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello Phil,<br><br>For calibration purpose,  I observed that you need minimum 3 items to go to the calibration ( at least for Rasch model)<br><br>I was experimenting with the LSAT7 data, which has 5 items in it. I calibrated first 3 items separately using Rasch 1 factor model.<br><br><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> raschfit</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mirt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;Rasch&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Iteration</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">83</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Lik</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1594.718</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Max</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Change</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.00010</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> coef</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">raschfit</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>$Item</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    a1     d g u<br>par  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.973</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>$Item</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    a1     d g u<br>par  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.839</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>$Item</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    a1     d g u<br>par  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.545</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>$GroupPars<br>    MEAN_1 COV_11<br>par      </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.436</span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Then I calibrated all 5 items together.<br><br><br><br></div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> raschfit_full</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mirt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;Rasch&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Iteration</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">27</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Lik</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2664.901</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Max</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Change</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.00010</span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> coef</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">raschfit_full</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>$Item</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    a1     d g u<br>par  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.868</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>$Item</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    a1     d g u<br>par  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.791</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>$Item</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    a1     d g u<br>par  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.461</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>$Item</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    a1     d g u<br>par  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.521</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>$Item</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    a1     d g u<br>par  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.993</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>$GroupPars<br>    MEAN_1 COV_11<br>par      </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.022</span></div></code></div><br>If you see there is change in the values of the parameter d. I have not changed the response pattern (or number of examinees) for first three items in both calibration.<br><br>Now I have a little bit more questions this time. :)<br><br><b>1)</b> Why there is a change in the values of the parameter?<br><br><b>2.A)</b> Another question which I have is that suppose I already have calibrated item bank of K items. I get M (K&gt;M) new uncalibrated items in future. Now what should be the good idea for calibrating these new items?</div><div><br></div><div><b>2.B)</b> Should we calibrate them separately? If yes what is the minimum number of items and responses required to get the robust item parameter estimates?<br><br></div><div><b>2.C)</b> Or should we calibrate them together? ( All K+M items are going together for calibration) ( but that might change the first K  items&#39; parameter estimates )<br><br>E.g. Suppose In a year I get some new items getting added to my item bank. So should I calibrate these M items at the end of the year when I have all the items.<br><br>Or I can go for the calibration as the items get added one by one (may be calibrating first  3 items, then second 3 and last 4 or first 5 and last 5) ?<br><br>My aim is to fetch the reliable estimates of item parameters.</div><div><br></div><div><b>3) </b> Last but not the least, Suppose I keep getting the more new responses to the already calibrated items ( through CAT (Computerized Adaptive Test)). These responses will be sparse as it is a CAT. Should I keep updating the item parameters by re-calibrating the items as long as I keep getting the new responses? Is it right that more the the responses better are item parameter estimates? Or should I stop at some point? If yes, what is that stopping criteria, where one can say that my item parameter estimates are robust?<br><br><br></div><div><br></div><div>Thanking you,<br>Irshad</div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><font color=\"#666600\"><div><br></div></font></div></code></div></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
